"""
## Purpose and audience

This specification is maintained for compiler and library engineers reviewing UNIT-COMPILER behavior, and for operators verifying a build.

## Operator workflow

Run `bin/simple test --no-session-daemon test/unit/compiler/typed_array_variable_binding_spec.spl` and confirm the Results line reports 0 failed. Each scenario names the requirement it traces to and starts with a Verify step.

## Compatibility and limitations

Covers only the behavior explicitly asserted below on the current toolchain. No external standard or cross-version contract is claimed; numeric oracles are explained inline where they appear.

## Recovery and troubleshooting

If a scenario fails, re-run the single spec file, read the first failing Verify step, and compare the reported value against the inline oracle comment before bisecting recent changes.
"""
# @manual: primary
# REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-001
# doc-path: doc/01_research/app/
# doc-path: doc/03_plan/app/
# doc-path: doc/04_architecture/app/
# doc-path: doc/05_design/app/
# @cover src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter 40%
# @req REQ-LANG-ARRAY-BIND-001
"""
# Copying a typed array between variables preserves its elements

**Category:** Language
**Status:** In Progress

## Purpose and Audience

Anyone who writes `val b = a` where `a` is a `[u64]` or `[u8]` expects `b` to
hold the same values. Today it does not: the copy keeps the correct length and
zeroes every element, with no error and no warning. This scenario exists so
that defect can never again be discovered by way of a wrong cryptographic
answer.

## Scope and Preconditions

Covers the value semantics of binding an existing typed-array variable to a new
name — `val`-to-`val`, `val`-to-`var`, `var`-to-`var`, and the array-literal
form. No host capability is required.

## Primary Workflow

A developer builds an array, binds it to another name, and reads an element
back. The element read must equal the element written, regardless of which
binding form carried it there.

## Key Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| Binding form | Whether the right-hand side is a call, a literal, or another variable |
| Length-preserving zeroing | The failure mode: `.length()` is right, every element is 0 |
| Swap idiom | `val t = a; a = b; b = t` — destructive while this defect is live |

## Evidence and Provenance

Measured 2026-08-17 on the deployed seed. Binding from a direct function call
returned 5 as written; every variable-to-variable binding returned 0 while
reporting length 2. Filed as
`doc/08_tracking/bug/typed_array_variable_binding_zeroes_elements_2026-08-17.md`.

## Recovery and Troubleshooting

While this is RED, do not treat a typed array as copyable by rebinding. Re-derive
the value through a function call at each use, and never rely on the three-step
swap idiom for arrays. A green run here means the workaround in
`src/lib/nogc_async_mut_noalloc/tls/x25519.spl` can be reverted to the direct
binding.

## Compatibility and Limitations

Asserts `[u64]`, the element type where the defect was measured. Other element
types are untested and may behave differently.
"""

use std.spec.*

# ============================================================================
# Helpers — the only binding form known to copy correctly is from a call, so
# every fixture originates there and the scenario varies what happens next.
# ============================================================================

fn _source() -> [u64]:
    [5u64, 6u64]

fn _first_via_val_to_val() -> i64:
    val original = _source()
    val copy = original
    copy[0].to_i64()

fn _first_via_val_to_var() -> i64:
    val original = _source()
    var copy = original
    copy[0].to_i64()

fn _first_via_var_to_var() -> i64:
    var original = _source()
    var copy = original
    copy[0].to_i64()

fn _first_via_literal_rebound() -> i64:
    val literal: [u64] = [3u64, 4u64]
    val copy = literal
    copy[0].to_i64()

fn _length_via_val_to_val() -> i64:
    val original = _source()
    val copy = original
    copy.length()

fn _swapped_second() -> i64:
    # The ordinary three-step swap. `moved_back` must end up holding what
    # `first` started with.
    var first = _source()
    var second: [u64] = [9u64, 8u64]
    val carried = first
    first = second
    second = carried
    second[0].to_i64()

# ============================================================================
# Specs
# ============================================================================

describe "A typed array copied to a new name keeps its elements":

    it "carries values through a val-to-val binding":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-001
            # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-002
            # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-003
        step("Verify: carries values through a val-to-val binding")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Build a [u64] whose first element is 5")
        step("Bind it to a second val and read element 0")
        expect(_first_via_val_to_val()).to_equal(5)  # oracle: expected value 5 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "carries values through a val-to-var binding":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-004
        step("Verify: carries values through a val-to-var binding")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Bind the same array to a var and read element 0")
        expect(_first_via_val_to_var()).to_equal(5)  # oracle: expected value 5 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "carries values through a var-to-var binding":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-005
        step("Verify: carries values through a var-to-var binding")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Bind a var array to another var and read element 0")
        expect(_first_via_var_to_var()).to_equal(5)  # oracle: expected value 5 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

    it "carries values when the source was an array literal":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-006
        step("Verify: carries values when the source was an array literal")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Bind a literal-initialised array to a new name and read element 0")
        expect(_first_via_literal_rebound()).to_equal(3)  # oracle: expected value 3 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

describe "Length alone does not prove a typed array copied correctly":

    it "reports the length that the elements should back":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-007
        step("Verify: reports the length that the elements should back")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        # Length survived the defect intact, so a length check is exactly the
        # assertion that would have missed it. It is asserted here as context
        # for the element checks above, never as a substitute for them.
        step("Confirm the copy reports two elements")
        expect(_length_via_val_to_val()).to_equal(2)  # oracle: expected value 2 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec

describe "The ordinary swap idiom moves values in both directions":

    it "leaves the second variable holding the first one's value":
        # @req: REQ-UNIT-COMPILER-008
        step("Verify: leaves the second variable holding the first one's value")
        # @capture: assertion outcomes retained in the spec Results output as evidence
        step("Swap two arrays through a temporary binding")
        step("Read element 0 of the variable that received the original")
        expect(_swapped_second()).to_equal(5)  # oracle: expected value 5 is the behavior asserted by this regression spec
